# TaskFlow auth flow — IntelliJ HTTP Client
#
# Run a single request with Ctrl+Enter, or run the whole file top to bottom.
# Login and Refresh capture their tokens automatically, so no token is ever pasted by hand.
#
# The app must be running. Requires APP_JWT_SECRET (at least 32 characters — HS256 rejects
# anything shorter) and PostgreSQL on localhost:5432/taskflow.

@host = http://localhost:8080

### Register
POST {{host}}/api/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "nam@example.com",
  "username": "xuannam",
  "password": "Password123"
}

### Login — captures both tokens
POST {{host}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "nam@example.com",
  "password": "Password123"
}

> {%
    client.global.set("accessToken", response.body.accessToken);
    client.global.set("refreshToken", response.body.refreshToken);
%}

### Current user
GET {{host}}/api/users/me
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}

### Rotate refresh token — captures the new pair
POST {{host}}/api/auth/refresh
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "refreshToken": "{{refreshToken}}"
}

> {%
    // Keep the token we just spent — the reuse test at the bottom needs it.
    client.global.set("oldRefreshToken", client.global.get("refreshToken"));
    client.global.set("accessToken", response.body.accessToken);
    client.global.set("refreshToken", response.body.refreshToken);
%}

### Logout current device
POST {{host}}/api/auth/logout
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}

{
  "refreshToken": "{{refreshToken}}"
}

### Logout all devices
POST {{host}}/api/auth/logout-all
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Bug hunting. Each request below should return a clean 401 with the error code
# named in the comment. Compare what you actually get against what you designed
# in ErrorCode and JwtAuthenticationEntryPoint.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

### No token at all — expect 401 AUTH_002
GET {{host}}/api/users/me

### Malformed token — expect 401 AUTH_002
GET {{host}}/api/users/me
Authorization: Bearer not-a-jwt

### Valid shape, wrong signature — expect 401 AUTH_002
# Header and payload are real, the signature is not.
GET {{host}}/api/users/me
Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9.eyJzdWIiOiIxIiwiaWF0IjoxNzAwMDAwMDAwLCJleHAiOjk5OTk5OTk5OTl9.wrong_signature_here

### Expired token — expect 401 AUTH_003
# Restart the app with APP_JWT_ACCESS_TOKEN_EXPIRATION=10s, run Login above,
# wait ten seconds, then run this.
GET {{host}}/api/users/me
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}

### Reusing an already-rotated refresh token — expect 401 AUTH_005
# Run Login, then Refresh, then this. It replays oldRefreshToken, the token that
# Refresh already revoked. Ask yourself the harder question afterwards: the token
# it replaced is still valid. Should it be?
POST {{host}}/api/auth/refresh
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "refreshToken": "{{oldRefreshToken}}"
}
